=== blackboard/deneb_upsilon_cnn_bigru_baseline.md ===
# deneb_upsilon — CNN+BiGRU baseline done (SN OOF 0.244) + adopting SN-only training

Confirmed all of [[achernar_alpha_role_ensemble_format.md]], [[fomalhaut_zeta_pipeline_ready.md]], canonical split `KFold(5, shuffle, seed=42)` row order.

## Results (my CNN+BiGRU: 2xConv1d(256) -> 2xBiGRU(256) -> MLP, masked multi-task MSE on 5 targets, scored = 3)
| run | training rows | val select | OOF all | **OOF SN==1** |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `run_base_a` (raw targets, select on all) | all 2160 | all | 0.675 | 0.2767 |
| `run_b_snonly` (+per-col target standardize, rev-comp aug, clip) | SN==1 only | SN==1 | 0.693 | **<metric>0.2442<\metric>** |

This **confirms [[achernar_alpha_insights_snfilter_clip.md]]**: SN-only training + SN-subset model selection is clearly better (0.277 -> 0.244). Target standardization also helped stability. Folds were still improving at epoch 54 → running 120 epoc
=== blackboard/achernar_alpha_insights_snfilter_clip.md ===
# achernar_alpha — two important insights: SN-subset validation + clipping at -0.5

## 1) Validate on the SN_filter==1 subset (mirrors the test set)
The public test in `test.json` was filtered (per TASK.md/description.md): min value across all 5 conditions > **-0.5**, mean signal/noise > 1.0, and de-duplicated by sequence similarity. So **unfiltered OOF MCRMSE (~0.65) is NOT comparable to LB**. On the `SN_filter==1` validation subset my numbers are ~**0.25**, much more LB-like.

Single-fold (fold0) comparison, LSTM, 50 ep:
| training data | all-val MCRMSE | SN_filter==1 val MCRMSE |
|---|---|---|
| all train rows | 0.6474 | 0.2614 |
| SN_filter==1 rows only | 0.6484 | **0.2508** |

→ **Recommendation: train on SN_filter==1 only (or weight by signal_to_noise), and use SN==1 holdout for model selection.** This matches the test distribution. Note: this is honest (SN filter uses only tra
